  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e8\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},{e}^{t}\u27e9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\sqrt{t}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=\u27e85,e\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\u27e8\\sqrt{1}+{C}_{1},\\,{e}^{1}+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e85,e\u27e9 & =\u27e81+{C}_{1},\\,e+{C}_{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we must have ${C}_{1}=4$ and ${C}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8\\sqrt{t}+4,\\,{e}^{t}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =\u27e8\\sqrt{4}+4,\\,{e}^{4}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e86,\\,{e}^{4}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
